<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phanariotes</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Phanariots</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Phanariotes were members of wealthy Greek families of the Phanar, the Greek quarter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecumenical-patriarch-of-constantinople">Ecumenical Patriarchate</a>. From the late 17th century they served the Ottoman state as dragomans, and from the 1710s the sultans appointed them princes of Moldavia and Wallachia, until the Greek revolt of 1821. Phanariotes also governed the autonomous Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> in the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phanariotes.jpg" alt="Phanariotes" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fanarion.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the coastal ports fell to the Ottomans in 1484 the economy turned inwards, and from the sixteenth century the sultans demanded grain, livestock and timber for Istanbul at fixed prices, a burden that grew heavy in the <b>Phanariot</b> period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After Ștefan Cantacuzino's execution in 1716 the Ottomans dropped the nominal elective system and appointed princes from the <b>Phanariotes</b> of Constantinople, a rule Nicholas Mavrocordatos had brought in 1715.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In November 1821 an assembly of local representatives met at Salona (Amfissa) and, under the guidance of the <b>Phanariot</b> diplomat Theodoros Negris, adopted a legal charter and created a governing council named after the ancient Athenian Areopagus.</blockquote><a href="/state/areopagus-of-eastern-continental-greece">Areopagus of Eastern Continental Greece</a></li><li><blockquote>In November 1821 the <b>Phanariot</b> politician Alexandros Mavrokordatos gathered local representatives at Missolonghi, where they adopted a charter and created a senate to govern the region and coordinate the military chieftains.</blockquote><a href="/state/organisation-of-western-continental-greece">Organisation of Western Continental Greece</a></li><li><blockquote>The prince, usually a <b>Phanariot Greek</b>, governed with an elected assembly and his own administration, flag and later postage stamps, from Vathy.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-samos">Principality of Samos</a></li></ul></section>
